    "title": "An untrusted prize contract can fabricate both escrow and delivery",
    "description": "The clean requirements treat arbitrary prize contracts as untrusted, but admission checks only that the contract reports ERC-721 interface support. During createRaffle, the prize contract controls safeTransferFrom: it can invoke the clone receiver with the expected caller-supplied arguments without maintaining real ownership, and the factory then asks that same untrusted contract whether the clone owns the token. This can activate a raffle with no genuine escrow. On an NFT-winning redemption, the same contract can make transferFrom a no-op and report the winner from ownerOf; the raffle then retains winnerRedeemed/prizeClaimed and the ticket burn while no genuine NFT was delivered. A sponsor controlling such a token can therefore sell entries and receive the NFT-outcome sponsor allocation without contributing an enforceable prize, contrary to the atomic-escrow and delivery requirements.\nAssumptions: The clean-design statement that arbitrary prize contracts are untrusted includes contracts that falsely advertise ERC-721 behavior; the interface prose's honest-prize assumption is not treated as an overriding trust assumption.; Entrants may buy from a raffle whose sponsor selected the adversarial prize contract.; The adversarial prize contract can return the ERC-721 interface ID and self-consistent but false ownerOf results while accepting transferFrom/safeTransferFrom calls.",
